Subject: [PATCH] Reject an unbounded Reshape input instead of dereferencing null

This packaging patch applies only to the sealed NPU compiler source
deb423b11b00af945fa788c9d38c51e30f0cf3d5 (npu_ud_2026_28_rc2). The
dynamic Reshape importer infers a missing output bound from the input
bounds. For an unbounded input the mlir::dyn_cast<BoundedTensorType>
returns null and the direct .getBounds() dereference terminates the
calling process (SIGSEGV) before the importer's post-walk rejection can
run. Check the cast and throw the same style of diagnostic used a few
lines above, so an unbounded model returns a normal API error.
Proposed upstream as openvinotoolkit/npu_compiler issue 352; the fix was
validated on hardware (issue archledger/intel-npu-stack 20): unbounded
NPU compilation changes from SIGSEGV to a catchable error and static,
bounded and plugin-batched cases are unchanged. The guard drops when an
upstream release carries the fix.

Regression: check-unbounded-reshape-guard.py refuses the unguarded
spelling in the prepared source and requires the checked form.

--- a/src/vpux_compiler/src/frontend/IE.cpp
+++ b/src/vpux_compiler/src/frontend/IE.cpp
@@ -2076,7 +2076,12 @@
                             "Reshape node '{0}': expected exactly 1 dynamic output bound dimension, got {1}", nodeName,
                             dynamicDimCount);
 
-            const auto& inputBounds = mlir::dyn_cast<Core::BoundedTensorType>(inputs[0].getType()).getBounds();
+            const auto boundedInputType = mlir::dyn_cast<Core::BoundedTensorType>(inputs[0].getType());
+            VPUX_THROW_WHEN(boundedInputType == nullptr,
+                            "Reshape node '{0}': input type carries no bounds, cannot infer the missing "
+                            "output bound",
+                            nodeName);
+            const auto& inputBounds = boundedInputType.getBounds();
             const int64_t inputBoundsProduct =
                     std::accumulate(inputBounds.begin(), inputBounds.end(), int64_t{1}, std::multiplies<int64_t>());
             const int64_t outputBoundsProduct =
